#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114982 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "c0c08150-98f9-5677-a7a8-390cffc0c5b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4511" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) =0$\n\n- The system ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/0b4ad54b702a4c7dc8cca849c32d035e558a8b91d8df22b14eb65b28f41093b0.png) has exactly one solution\n\n- $A^{-1}$ does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/79f95782298c108b174ce190af7b3a073fa0f704072932967c4f16313c955ce7.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114983 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "8c1e5afa-0afa-56ee-bbfa-4e84c64093ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4512" :question/problem "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/15ba3333a2ced8790efd81a35d26be43d2f0841b9529bf9d6b5b5e1c0e9f9b67.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/18978e96f80b0ba3d0f997c1db6162886b9d9e2d2c5ee7d5925a48b6147081af.png) where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n\n- The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/e49dcc022775cc8eb5317611a3fae4d21892a7c566989f67f65e5213ee5c6b9d.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =k-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4.$ \n\nSo, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/id #uuid "cb19dcff-bb9d-55dc-941b-48153ca62e7a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4513" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/db710af5540e8c811c83c12c23a5f5289539fc8628a936c183badc00933b2068.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "921ae110-e4e7-510f-ab59-186a0cb3c973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330494} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7c9b25-12fc-5ac1-805f-8e0c3b86794d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and  III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330495} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2491c8-3992-5033-a799-225d97c66391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and  III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330496} {:answer/id #uuid "6e261158-2749-58a8-a570-b61fcbae7353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330497} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae7a3d7-f990-5ac5-bbf2-932a489a4383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ae7a3d7-f990-5ac5-bbf2-932a489a4383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330498} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0892a77-ce44-577c-a9a2-1c997811c395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a109e6d7-dc35-5583-9b59-06b02bed5042" :question/math-academy-id "q-127918" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-11 \\\\ y+7x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The coefficient matrix is not invertible\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not zero\n- The lines $x+2y=-11$ and $y+7x=4$ intersect at infinitely many  points" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcfb8ec-67b9-52c2-a103-85ed57f59a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330515} {:answer/id #uuid "95d1279f-cb6e-59d1-bd60-616e11825c41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has \n no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330516} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc6ae29-26fc-5d1d-836f-a161036544a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330517} {:answer/id #uuid "a4847cea-f8f4-5525-bf6d-3821634688e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is the solution to $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330518} {:answer/id #uuid "f096ebb6-a1ba-569e-9941-f0b783e9cf4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bcfb8ec-67b9-52c2-a103-85ed57f59a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330515} :answer-field/id #uuid "deefcdbd-7194-5ea6-b70b-6b8a393ac02c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d9e985e-33c1-5d2f-8e17-571966f68cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-128154" :question/problem "Consider the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be4f38e4-f95b-510a-b64d-a450503108a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113379} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb86065-d517-56b5-a8ee-c248f9a31ce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113380} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4d0aed-dfc5-5642-b876-2a17d8c41054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113381} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9ea779-207c-57b6-9a20-501e8fd04629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113382} {:answer/id #uuid "6407f499-bcd8-55cc-8e0a-812c59d2271e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b9ea779-207c-57b6-9a20-501e8fd04629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113382} :answer-field/id #uuid "63fd17cf-7b7f-5f83-bf00-93978ee2775b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113378}] :question/id #uuid "947396a7-1dd0-5962-800b-c42f03f930a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-32091" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n${3x + \\sqrt{7}y = 0.3; - 2\\sqrt{0.1}x + 3y = \\sqrt{7}$.\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The lines $3x + \\sqrt{7}y = 0.3$ and $-2\\sqrt{0.1}x + 3y = \\sqrt{7}$ intersect exactly at one point\n2. The determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $0$\n3. The coefficient matrix is invertible"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a53765dd-7014-5b39-bf54-e8f3976b2384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113409} {:answer/id #uuid "13322188-8978-5455-b6c9-479a518c6596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113410} {:answer/id #uuid "22096abe-1943-50f3-99f0-aa1dafa8ad8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113411} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca7fc4f-bcc4-5cd1-80f3-0af1032a7077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113412} {:answer/id #uuid "55d76481-214e-51c0-a915-5a564235ade6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13322188-8978-5455-b6c9-479a518c6596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113410} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d8db78-0edf-543a-9a4c-fbbba37ddad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e42babae-3f60-5ad6-9c62-eaf91af5f106" :question/math-academy-id "q-32167" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and III give us true statements:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $det(A)\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a66e2ad3-9381-528b-8d62-fef833a90543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113704} {:answer/id #uuid "499006b0-f8ab-55ca-becd-4d4b299aacca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113705} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc83cc9-86c9-581c-971d-0e0a533005c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113706} {:answer/id #uuid "79320fee-2d9b-5b07-881c-392096f745eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113707} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd90075-27ee-52c3-a192-1879fc3137c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79320fee-2d9b-5b07-881c-392096f745eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113707} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8794eb2-5786-5bcf-a6ce-2ede11c4aa02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f74e266c-1efa-53d7-993c-e40799d4cc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-36073" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283cd890-ca0a-56c2-8503-e089690a9d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330563} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc81105-ed1e-5bd2-8715-12b26afd8439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330564} {:answer/id #uuid "45dc26a5-3586-538a-8d9f-4e6e6947b389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330565} {:answer/id #uuid "6903117f-7bfc-5291-82cf-a8f525f7d6e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330566} {:answer/id #uuid "230bf8f7-ce78-538b-9148-626c032770de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dc81105-ed1e-5bd2-8715-12b26afd8439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330564} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4336250-6373-548c-93ca-b2653e9b0d03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e36a6e4b-b2b2-5f73-af06-061f4729b3a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73565" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem: \n\n- Statement II is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e2ddf0-f804-5a30-980d-66304828d094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351567} {:answer/id #uuid "5be870e4-e9bd-5937-987a-d79308c5cc3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351568} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2b59cd-65d9-5d5c-8608-f9aa1b253dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ are parallel" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351569} {:answer/id #uuid "81b9a644-f5fb-5579-a1e9-3121dea65ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution for the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351570} {:answer/id #uuid "bca0fdcf-aa0b-5275-8400-08e14037c3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca0fdcf-aa0b-5275-8400-08e14037c3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351571} :answer-field/id #uuid "266ea60b-4a4f-549a-9d0f-158bc22cdf50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b78864d-df1c-54a9-a660-cac490a85eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73608" :question/problem "Consider the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$"}]}
